<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Trim warnings of the Windows SDK projection (docs/EVENTS.md §6.4, task W3), passed to ILLink with
  link-attributes by Thirteen.Desktop.csproj for the Windows target only.

  What warns: publishing the Windows target trimmed (TrimMode=full, warnings as errors) reports 35 IL2081
  warnings, all inside the projection that ships with the .NET SDK (Microsoft.Windows.SDK.NET.Ref
  10.0.19041.57, CsWinRT 2.x): WinRT.Runtime and Microsoft.Windows.SDK.NET. Every one is in a static
  constructor's "fallback" local function of a generic ABI marshaler (ABI.System.Collections.Generic.
  IReadOnlyList<T>, IDictionary<K,V>, KeyValuePair<K,V>, ABI.Windows.Foundation.IAsyncOperation<T>,
  IObservableMap<K,V>, ...), which builds the marshaler of a generic WinRT interface at run time with
  Activator.CreateInstance when no compile-time marshaler was generated for that instantiation. They are
  reached by the projection's own module initializer as soon as the app uses any WinRT type, so they
  appear with the toast code alone (and also came from CommunityToolkit.Mvvm's Windows build, which
  carries a CsWinRT vtable lookup).

  Why suppressing is safe for Thirteen: the only WinRT calls are the toast scheduler's (ToastPlatform.cs):
  ToastNotificationManager.CreateToastNotifier, XmlDocument.LoadXml, ScheduledToastNotification,
  ToastNotifier.AddToSchedule / RemoveFromSchedule (no generics), and
  ToastNotifier.GetScheduledToastNotifications, whose IReadOnlyList<ScheduledToastNotification> is
  marshaled by the helper the projection generates for its own signatures, not by a fallback; and the
  text size (TextScaling.cs): new UISettings() and its TextScaleFactor, a double. No Thirteen object
  crosses into WinRT. Every scheduler call is inside try/catch, so even a fallback that
  failed at run time would turn the scheduler off for the session (as a missing app id does), never
  crash the app. The suppression is limited to IL2081 in these two assemblies; any other trim warning,
  and any warning in Thirteen's own code or another package, still fails the publish. The VM check
  (Thirteen.exe dash dash clear-alerts on the trimmed build, windows/README.md) exercises that call.
  Remove this file when the SDK's projection stops reporting them (CsWinRT 3 is trim-clean).
-->
<linker>
  <assembly fullname="WinRT.Runtime">
    <attribute fullname="System.Diagnostics.CodeAnalysis.UnconditionalSuppressMessageAttribute">
      <argument>Trimming</argument>
      <argument>IL2081</argument>
      <property name="Scope">module</property>
      <property name="Justification">CsWinRT 2.x generic ABI fallbacks; Thirteen marshals no generic WinRT interface without a generated helper (ILLink.WindowsSdk.xml).</property>
    </attribute>
  </assembly>
  <assembly fullname="Microsoft.Windows.SDK.NET">
    <attribute fullname="System.Diagnostics.CodeAnalysis.UnconditionalSuppressMessageAttribute">
      <argument>Trimming</argument>
      <argument>IL2081</argument>
      <property name="Scope">module</property>
      <property name="Justification">CsWinRT 2.x generic ABI fallbacks; Thirteen marshals no generic WinRT interface without a generated helper (ILLink.WindowsSdk.xml).</property>
    </attribute>
  </assembly>
</linker>
